course: dictionary
lesson: 9075
title: Slogans: short, catchy and persuasive
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A slogan is a short, catchy phrase. Adverts use slogans to make you remember a product, and groups use them to say what they want. Read this: «Every little helps.» It is short and easy to remember, and it makes you think of a shop, not a list of prices.
explain: Slogans try to persuade you. Look at the words around a slogan to see what it wants you to do or feel. «Adverts use persuasive language and slogans, sometimes slang to sound friendly to young people.» Slang like «bussin'» makes an advert sound like it is speaking to you.
explain: A slogan is not the same as the full message. «A slogan is a short catchy phrase; the full list of promises is in a party's manifesto, which most people never read.» The slogan is the quick part. The long, detailed part is somewhere else.
explain: When you make your own slogan, keep it short and clear, and check it. Say it out loud. Does it make people remember your idea? Is it a fact, or only an opinion like «the best pizza in town»?
fact: A slogan is a short, catchy phrase.
fact: Slogans are used in advertising, and by groups running a campaign or protest.
fact: A slogan is meant to be remembered, so it is usually short and has a good rhythm.
fact: «The best pizza in town» is an opinion, not a checked fact.
fact: A manifesto is the full list of a party's promises; a slogan is only the short, catchy version.
exercise: «Every little helps» is a famous slogan. What is a slogan? | *A short, catchy phrase used to make people remember a product or an idea | A type of cereal | A long list of rules | A question asked in a test
exercise: Protesters carry signs with a slogan on them. What is the slogan for? | To make a list of promises | To count how many people are present | *To show the aims of their campaign | To sell cereal
exercise: What is a slogan trying to do in an advert? | *To persuade us and help us remember the product | To make us fall asleep | To give us the full list of facts | To show the price only
test: Which of these is an opinion, not a checked fact? | "a short catchy phrase" | "the sun rises in the east" | *"the best pizza in town" | "twelve months in a year"
test: Mia is making a slogan for a recycling campaign. Which one is most like a slogan? | *Recycle today, and our planet will thank you | Bins are blue and bins are green | The lorry comes on Tuesday | Paper is made from trees
exam: Tom wants a slogan for the school sports team. Which is most like a slogan? | The match starts at ten o'clock | The pitch is muddy after the rain | Bring your boots tomorrow | *Go Eagles, go for gold!
